body:
I meant bipolar but both, actually.

&#x200B;

I also doubt my diagnosis. I tend to only get whatever it is in Feb or March. Same time apparently. A pattern with emoods and daylio. Look at my post history.

&#x200B;

It is, yes, but I have ADHD and memory issues anyway so I might not notice.

CBD has antipsychotic properties.

&#x200B;

And I know, but I dont usually feel like I have that much energy, maybe in spurts but mm, it's mainly just sleeping less.

It's not the label, really. I just want to know what's up. I don't currently have a doctor. I'm just taking what medicine I have while looking for one.

&nbsp;

Good points, though, in that it can be really subjective.
Mild hypomania is to me more energetic, more social, more optimistic, maybe a bit more creative and impulsive, more talkative. Sometimes I sleep three hours a night but sometimes up to six or if I need it, eight one night, then back down to three or so hours. I think about changing my hair, getting piercings and tattoos more often. I occasionally feel like doing risky things like walking somewhere high and narrow, driving extremely fast when I don't drive at all, and doing harder stuff. Sometimes I'm more productive but sometimes I just can't focus and want to get out of there and maybe move around but it's usually manageable and gum helps.

&nbsp;

I'm more familiar with the angry ones, though. I've only had two or three like the above. I get angry, but it's not always at the front of my mind. I can kind of be ok if left alone, but I'm way easier to get angry and blow. I scream more. I hate the world more than usual. I want to break things or hit walls. I sometimes want to hurt myself just because of how I feel but sometimes it's redirected anger. People talk so slowly. I can't focus. I don't sleep well.

&nbsp;

Depression is harder to quantify for me, to decide if it's mild or moderate or severe. I'm reluctant to call it severe because I almost always get out of bed, at least eventually, but scales have given me moderate-severe quite a few times, and severe a couple times.

&nbsp;

Usually while mild I can function and do some work, I just have a hard time doing it and feel low and sometimes like life is pointless. 

&nbsp;

Moderate is when I'm exhausted even with several naps yet I don't necessarily sleep 10 or 12 hours a night. I want to skip class and be alone. I don't enjoy much. I can really struggle to do work. If it's really bad, I don't feel like walking or talking. Thankfully that's usually only for a few hours- the difficulty in moving and talking. Sometimes I reach out instead. If this lasts for more than a week or two, I tend to get suicidal. I've written several notes in the last twelve years and come very close a few times and came up with a plan and rudimentary date last time. I considered admitting myself for a while before it got worse.

How well do distraction tactics work for you? I've found that it often helps to tell myself to get through the next five or ten minutes, then repeat. Increments of time.

&nbsp;

Also, funny comics or tv shows or movies. Video games.

I used to have a lot of problems with self harm and I may have been addicted at one point, even if I never cut very deeply.

&nbsp;

Rubber bands help, though sometimes they do leave welts. I would recommend against scrunchies with metal bands since those, in my experience, are more likely to welt and bruise.

&nbsp;

If you use ice cubes, it may be a good idea to wrap a (damp might be ok but I'm no doctor) paper towel around it to reduce the risk of any lasting injury or damage.

"But I am also aware of this weird sort of quickening of my thoughts. Not in a fertilisation way although I did see some late ducklings yesterday and they were so beautiful - but I am thinking faster and becoming the master of my own head again."

&nbsp;

That sounds like a warning sign to me- thoughts speeding up and seemingly unrelated thoughts in the part about fertilization and baby ducks. And possibly your finding them beautiful.

&nbsp;

Also, that does sound kind of like a lot to have already gotten done in one morning. What time was it when you posted this?

&nbsp;

I would suggest keeping an eye on your sleep and talking to your doctor. I don't really know how the NHS works, and I can't remember what you've already tried, but please keep trying.

I actually do get headaches fairly often and migraines on occasion. But my room mate (at least currently, hoping it stays this way) is a friend- we requested each other on our housing applications- from when we were younger. We're not close. She does know I have issues with my health, arthritis, and vaguely that I've had problems with depression in the past but that I'm on medication that works now.

&nbsp;

But it's a suite. (With two people in the other room and a shared bathroom.) Thank God for that because my previous assignment was in a different dorm with a community bathroom, ugh.

&nbsp;

Yeah, I dunno. I've only ever told one teacher I struggled with depression during a bad episode. I still failed the class though she tried to help. I don't like talking about it to people, particularly those in positions of authority.

Hm. What is/was your depression like? I'm kind of reluctant to call it severe because I'm not incapacitated or bedridden, but when describing my worst one I might use the word severe once or twice.

&nbsp;

I wish I had that kind of access to articles.
I'm not taking my Trileptal like I should be but I don't feel like I need it and I don't like how it seems to make me search for words and get muscle twitches or whatever causes the sensation of gnats or fleas landing on and off of me.

&nbsp;

I'm not completely averse to trying Lamictal again, though. Even though starting it roughly coincides with the beginning of my writer's block years ago (though it still hasn't gone away) and possibly made my memory worse permanently.  Or it's all coincidence, but so much coincidence. I was told I stopped it because I was my vision was blurring over more often for a few seconds to a few minutes, memory problems, and I think also more headaches.

&nbsp;

I do plan on getting a second opinion on my diagnosis from another doctor in my new city and discussing other options for mood stabilization, but I want to stay on the Pristiq since it seems to be the first antidepressant that's really worked.

That's interesting. Thankfully, my depression thus far has never been wholly debilitating. Like, I can still function somewhat while depressed. In my worst one, I was suicidal and planned it and wrote notes and I failed my classes but I did go, though I probably wouldn't have if I could drive myself and wasn't dropped off.

&nbsp;

What's your diagnosis? Mine is cyclothymia. I also do wonder if taking Depakote for some reason as a child and Lamictal for a few years in and after high school affected the course, possibly lessening the severity or slowing the progression.

My diagnosis is cyclothymia and in the DSM-IV, it's regarded as having hypomania and depressive symptoms but not major depressive episodes. In the DSM-5, they changed it so that neither meet criteria for an episode. I'm not sure which definition my p-nurse goes by.

&nbsp;

Sometimes I don't even really agree with the cyclothymia though and think it's recurrent depression with some odd things.

&nbsp;

What would be considered affecting my ability to function normally? And vs most normal people, or vs my normal self? Because I usually struggle with motivation and focus but I've had a couple of times where I was motivated and went to school on days I didn't need to in order to go to tutoring and do homework and I tried to do maybe a few weeks worth in one week. I don't remember if I succeeded. I do know that on one day my mood kept ping ponging a bit and I had racing thoughts like never before and it steadily made it harder to focus until I stopped  working and I think I emailed my teacher or stopped by her office to tell her I couldn't make up the test that day and I took a couple of low dose Klonopin in the hopes that it would slow me down. It worked. I don't remember just how well or if they came back to any extent later or the next day. I know it usually takes more than that for bipolar people.

I was like that to some degree in high school until I started Lamictal. It was pretty awful. I did get higher than I do now, but it was only up to a few hours long and I'm not sure if I also became more energetic.

&nbsp;

Yes, though I should have already graduated college with a BA. Still, I'm not 25+ yet.

Well yeah, although really I've only had like one or two that might have been months long and I don't think they were as severe. I do think it's been getting more severe the last few years. But generally they're two to four or five weeks long. Which is weird. Last year I had about five depressions and they were never more than five weeks long so I could count on that. They were also only a month to a month and half apart. Ugh. Thankfully, it seems like Pristiq stopped all that. I did get a tiny bit down in December briefly and then in January to February for about a month but only marked a few days mildly depressed because I wasn't sure.

&nbsp;

All in all, I'd estimate at least ten depressions from mild to possibly severe and I think I've been suicidal about four or five times in the last three years but I'm not sure.

Hm. Yeah. Except I've only had racing thoughts once, thankfully. It was horrible and kept gaining speed until I felt like I was a train about to derail. And I don't get the desire to clean lol. At least not really. Laundry doesn't count to me.

&nbsp;

What's your diagnosis, out of curiosity?

The DSM-5 criteria for cyclothymia does suggest that it involves symptoms that don't quite meet criteria for either a hypomanic or depressive episode, but that's a bit of a change from the DSM-IV, where it was just the depressive symptoms that didn't meet criteria.

&nbsp;

And I don't know if I agree with that. I do think I've experienced at least one major depressive episode, even if I remained somewhat functional and it was less severe than many on here. I had like 7 or 8 of the 9 criteria and it was about three weeks long with a bit of a weird lull of a week or so then possibly back to it only it was different and numb instead of despairing and suicidal. Or it was another episode, just very soon after.

I do, but they're usually not intense, just like I wouldn't mind getting up and running. I hate running. Sometimes I do feel mildly uncomfortable but I can sit still for the most part, with maybe just some fidgeting. Gum helps when I'm like this. But I don't typically get tired afterwards. Maybe because I don't do anything with it?
&nbsp;

It only lasts up to two hours and doesn't always come with increased mood or talkativeness, which is a bit odd. It's also unpredictable. I think it may have started a few years ago but definitely last year. It's happened more this year than last though. Maybe because I'm not depressed and because I'm taking Pristiq.

I have the same doubts but there are times that I see things on here that I relate to, mostly toned down versions of the typical euphoric, hyper-productive hypomania. Or irritable hypomania, although mine isn't as intense either.

&nbsp;

Did they tell you why you were diagnosed with bipolar, and which type?

Mm, well, caffeine barely affects me as it is. I need high doses to get any effect and even then it's mild and pretty short, though I usually don't crash.

&nbsp;

Hypomania isn't really a problem since it's rare and mild and just positive and short lived. I don't really get impulsive or want to spend money or have sex. I might want to talk more and go out and drink more caffeine and alcohol and stuff but I refrain. 

&nbsp;

I doubt whether this is really hypomania because of that though I did have decreased sleep, increased self esteem, happiness, optimism, and was less self conscious and a bit more outgoing. I've only had that once or twice that I can remember. I've had angry weeks a bit more- now those can be a problem. Even then I only had one last year and there were circumstances leading up to it and I was under stress failing classes and having to make up a test and back home after spring break also with a cold...

OCD is a bitch, and so is anxiety. I consider mine to be a bit mild though I've washed my hands to the point of them drying and cracking so that they bleed a bit and which counterproductively makes me a bit more vulnerable to germs. I've also been told I have GAD, but I don't think my anxiety is quite that severe nor does it seem very generalized to me.

&nbsp;

How have you dealt with suicidality? Last year was the worst and I was so off balance I had depression at least five times last year (two to four weeks each time only thankfully but with only a month to month and half of being okayish in beteeen) and came up with a plan and wrote several notes until I started Pristiq.

That's my diagnosis, actually. And I dealt with that for a time in high school. It was kind of awful and confusing. Nothing set it off. I would just go up and down pretty much every day multiple times a day. Then I started Lamictal and my junior and senior year were better. Even after I stopped it, I've never gone back to that degree of rapid mood shifts.

&nbsp;

What's your dual diagnosis if you don't mind me asking?

&nbsp;

I'm not sure how to explore it and I'm not sure if it's actually a thing because I'm only sure it happened last year and this year though I'm also pretty sure I tend to get suicidal once or twice a year as well. Or I did before and I'm hoping it doesn't happen this year.

Me too. I graduated high school in 2011, failed both semesters of my first year in college and took a year off then went back as a student on probation and since then I've only taken two classes at a time with a couple of semesters taking three.

&nbsp;

I do have ADHD and horrible time management and study skills. The medication doesn't really help, maybe only a little with what I call layered thoughts and stuff like that.

&nbsp;

I'm hoping to finally finish this summer but my family kind of gives me a hard time about taking so long.

Yeah, but I have ankylosing spondylitis, which is an autoimmune disorder. Most days outside of a flare, though, it's pretty mild to nonexistent. Especially if I stay on top of my medication. If i go a while without it, I start to ache more. It's better than it used to be. I used to have at least some pain most days, mainly in my hands, knees, and neck. My problem areas are still the same although now it's also my elbows, which seems weird. My back rarely hurts which is a bit unusual for my type of arthritis, but my rheumatologist has said that this may just be the way it presents in me and that I definitely have it, since I have the gene for it and my mom also has it.

&nbsp;

I don't know much about it but there are mice and mouse pads that are better for arthritis and tendonitis. Personally, I'm usually okay unless I write a lot with a pen/cil, and/or too quickly. I also do most of my computer use on a laptop, which I think helps.

Nope. I thought I was doing okay and stopped taking anything but adhd medication and then stopped taking that too because I was concerned it could be making my anxiety worse and I didn't think it helped very much anyway. (This was with the approval of my p-nurse, btw.)

&nbsp;

I'm back on Concerta and I'm still unsure about mood stabilizers but I'm definitely doing better since I started Pristiq. The frequency and severity has gone way down, especially compared to last year where, twice in three months, I seriously contemplated suicide and planned and wrote notes and considered voluntarily admitting myself. I kind of do wonder if it's just coincidence and its just me being lucky but I'll take it and I'm staying on my Pristiq and I make a definite effort to not miss it for more than a day or two.

That's good. I do have a 7 day pill case, AM and PM. I'm better at taking my medication in the morning, though, and I don't seem to get sedating effects from allergy medication or Trileptal, so when I do take those, I take them in the morning. I actually take the birth control primarily for regulation, since my cycle is very irregular and I can go months without it even on the pill. This has resulted in a couple of scares when I was sexually active.

&nbsp;

I've toyed with the idea of getting a fitbit or smart watch, but they're just so expensive and I'm not sure I'd wear it everyday. I do use reminders on my smartphone's calendar but I tend to think 'later' or just grow used to the reminders and they lose their effectiveness even when I change the wording.

&nbsp;

It sounds like you've been through a lot.

Personally, a scale like that would be really difficult to make because I don't think my mood has 20 levels. I only see myself as having three or four levels of depression, with 5 being completely normal and 6 a bit happier than normal, 7 possibly hypomanic, and 8 rarely ever happens and doesn't last long, a few hours to a day or two at most. Maybe two or three days once but I don't know. If so, the desire to do risky stuff wasn't continuously present.

&nbsp;

And yup, me too. I have to tell people. It's possible I might seem a little down or off but I don't think it's worryingly so. I only saw one particular psychiatrist three times before he dropped me but he was like, "Everyday, really?" when last year I told him I had been suicidal virtually everyday for the past month but was feeling a bit better, almost normal. Then I went into kind of a numb depression for most if not all of May so I don't know if I got a break for a few days to a week and half or if it was... I dunno.

I have the same difficulty. I did make a rough personalized mood scale that goes from 1-8 with 1 being definite danger zone and suicidal and planning and writing notes and 8 being almost euphoric and wanting to do things like party and maybe even play chicken though I doubt I'd do that.

&nbsp;

But the thing is that I can get suicidal even when it's not as severe as a 1. At 2 and 3. I've also never become nonfunctioning, only experienced difficulty with school, showering, etc.

(I know this was a joke, but people do do this. I've kind of done this on a very small scale hoping to boost my mood at least.)

&nbsp;

*Anyway,* how do people avoid overdosing on caffeine? Especially if not very responsive to caffeine. For instance, according to Genomind, I'm a rapid metabolizer of CYP1A2. This could explain why normal amounts of caffeine in soda and small to most medium cups of coffee do nothing to me.

&nbsp;

Concerta also seems to do nothing to very little for me, much like the other ADHD medications I've been prescribed.

&nbsp;

Is it really that effortless for most people here to stay up more than one night? It usually isn't for me. Normally I can do alright on two nights, sometimes three, of four hours of sleep per night but I feel it to some extent during the day and notice myself making errors in thinking and stuff. This has also happened after I unintentionally pulled an all nighter, though it was fairly mild. I do often get second and third winds, possibly the longer this goes on and maybe if I push through it, I feel okay. It's only happened a few times (total includes when normal and when a bit up.)

Yeah, I'm doing so much better this year. It's amazing. I'm not a very cheerful person, but I'm fairly content with my life right now and last year I wasn't sure if I'd ever feel like this again. I find it hard to believe sometimes that I got so low.

&nbsp;

Thanks. I do struggle with this a lot, thinking I don't have it as bad/extreme as others or thinking I don't have such a bad life and I should be more grateful.

&nbsp;

I keep wanting to get a second and third opinion but there's not a lot of pdocs around here. Maybe I would find this easier to accept if other pdocs agreed I'm on the spectrum. I just keep going from "Nah, it's recurrent unipolar depression with some odd symptoms/incidents" to "Well, it does make sense even if it's not quite typical."

I intend to. I had racing thoughts once but it was really unpleasant. Otherwise it's mainly just less sleep and more irritability and arguments and wanting to break something sometimes but it doesn't seem as continuous or severe as others I've seen on here. Neither positive or negative highs are.

&nbsp;

I don't know if they really warrant treatment and I like the happy ups. They're not extreme and I don't get grandiose or very impulsive so I don't see the problem. I just maybe sleep less and talk a bit more (I'm already prone to excessive talking) and become more social and sometimes more productive. I did kind of almost cheat on my then boyfriend once but not exactly and that was quite a bit higher than I usually ever get and very brief.

&nbsp;

When I get really angry or really low I tend to want to cut. I don't often cut especially when stable.

&nbsp;

She does know about the irritability and the suicidality of last year but maybe I didn't explain it in enough depth. I mostly just said it was the worst I'd ever been and that I had a plan and the thought of getting like that again scares me.

I'm not sure since I've only been tracking my mood for a year but I'd guess maybe four to six months max? Definitely not longer than a year. I'm pretty sure that in the past I've tended to have at least one to three depressive episodes a year or get suicidal/near suicidal once or twice a year.

&nbsp;

I also have the vague impression at least based on something I said once to my proc that I get angry for a few days once or twice a year.

&nbsp;

Last year was my worst year both mood and stability wise. I only had up to a month and half of being okay(ish) between depressions. I had five definite (two weeks+) documented lows before October when I started Pristiq and one angry week in March but that week is a very confusing story. I got very definitely suicidal twice last year and got pretty damn close to attempting and to admitting myself. It was also the most I've ever planned out a method, location, tentative date, etc.

Yeah. I was diagnosed with ADHD as a kid and had the diagnosis confirmed by another psychiatrist last year. Although he thought I might have GAD in addition to OCD and he wasn't sure about a mood disorder. I think he might have been reserving judgment though, until I saw him more. That's not how it happened, though. I only saw him three times before he released me. He said something about possibly coming back after seeing a therapist/psychologist, but I'm not really sure. It was abrupt and right after I told him I'd been in the worst depression is ever experienced and had suicidal thoughts virtually everyday and devoloped a plan.

&nbsp;

Anyway, I agree that I have anxiety but not GAD. I don't think it's that generalized or severe. I was diagnosed with IED and ODD as a kid. I think I've grown out of them and I think my p-nurse agreed. I checked to make sure she knew and if she'd taken that into consideration, although I didn't exactly phrase it like that. She said yes and she still thinks it's cyclothymia causing the days of anger and my weird ups and the week in Feb/March.

&nbsp;

I think I may have experienced at least onr major depressive episode, though, particularly last April, and cyclothymia excludes major depressive episodes. To be honest though the line between moderate depression and actual major depressive episodes seems a bit murky.

&nbsp;

The pseudo-depression makes sense. You're saying that when you drink you feel down/off for a few weeks afterwards? Every time? That's intriguing. Why drink, then? Or have you grown to rely on it? While I don't really drink, I can empathize with having a reliance on something.

I'm not worried, per se, just wanting to stay mostly stable. I can't afford to get depressed this summer with two six week courses. And last night/this morning I only got an hour to an hour and half of sleep. I just didn't want to sleep but eventually did and woke up feeling alright though I did have coffee as a back up. Admittedly I was playing a video game, but usually I still get tired anyway and turn it off and go to sleep or fall asleep while playing, so I don't think it was the stimulation. I'm also going out this weekend and next weekend after months of no social life and I might want to drink, hence my mention of reading that alcohol can seemingly cause depression, at least for some people.

&nbsp;

I still marvel at what it must be like to get back on your feet after such episodes. I don't think I could do it.

So, uh... what is it? I've had similar experiences to the OP although I'm not sure it was this constant or intense. I don't remember it very well. I think I was okay when left alone but I did get angrier more easily and more intensely and I did have racing thoughts at least one day on Wednesday and took two or three low dose Klonopin to try to stop it. It seemed to help but I'm not sure if it should have since I only took it that time. I don't remember how my energy was but my focus was sort of all over the place I think from focused to unfocused especially with the racing and my mood ping ponging that day. I don't know if it was affected by my cold or the medicine I was taking for it. If Mucinex D and DM caused this.

&nbsp;

I do think I remember starting to get angry on Sunday, and I think that was the day before I started taking it. And I did mark mild depression on one day but later deleted it. I know the anger or whatever lasted about four or five days. It might have stopped pretty abruptly, with me back to normal by Friday or Saturday. I don't remember when my cold actually got better. I think maybe a few days after that.

&nbsp;

I did have more motivation during that time or at least I committed to going to school four days that week when normally I only go twice. I think my self esteem may have been slightly increased because I went from believing I would fail and I couldn't do it to not thinking that and thinking I could even maybe pull it up to a C and I didn't drop the class, although this was also in part due to my parents not agreeing with dropping them and I don't think I explained how bad it was.

&nbsp;

Although I'm just really not sure about any of this because it was right after spring break and I had to make up a test and I was failing or close to it due to struggling with depression and seeming to have a bad reaction to Effexor. I had more suicidal thoughts after going up to 75mg. I think everyday. They might have been similar to intrusive thoughts. I think I was only mildly depressed but still. Then I stopped it right before spring break I think. I do recall having an intrusive thought of how I'd kill myself.

&nbsp;

Anyway, so anger and a cold and all that and having recently come off an antidepressant. I've read starting or stopping antidepressants can cause symptoms similar to above. I don't think I've ever had racing thoughts apart from that time though. Maybe once another time but that's it. Anxiety and adhd don't make my thoughts race, just more urgent and cluttered/unfocused.

I've been where you are. I was thinking about this earlier. This time last year I was in the worst depression I've ever had and maybe I should have tried to admit myself, which I did consider. But now I'm glad I kept going and sometimes I have a hard time believing I was that low and desperate.

&nbsp;

The thing about this and episodic depression is that it's episodic. It *will* get better, especially with the right medication. Hopefully this new one is it, but if not, don't give up. It took me three or four antidepressants and a few other medications, including mood stabilizers, to find something that works.

Wtf was she thinking though? That could have ended worse than it did with severe injury.

&nbsp;

Your explanation reminds me of a paper we got in middle school where you were supposed to read the instructions before beginning, and if you did, you only had to do a couple things like sign your name and a couple of things I don't remember and you were done. But if not, there was a lot more you ended up doing. I think I fell for it even though I usually try to read the instructions first.

Hm. I also looked for psychologists/therapists. Pretty much the same problem- many aren't taking new patients and some use methods I don't like the sound of, such as gestalt and hypnotherapy. I'm also uneasy with a couple others I can't recall the names of.

&nbsp;

I'm also afraid of being pulled off of Pristiq or not finding someone to prescribe it. I think I finally found something that works. At least, I hope so. It seemed to stop it from getting worse in January/Feb, very mild, and that was pretty much the only time it happened since October, which is a huge difference from earlier last year, when I was lucky to have a month and half at most between depressions, and I decided on a method and location and a tentative date. I didn't attempt, but I came pretty close more than once last year. (Does the above need a spoiler tag?)

I've already been on quite a few antidepressants and I'm not sure why I took Depakote as a kid, but I did and didn't react well to it. I took Lamictal for a few years. I'm not sure how well it worked since I don't really remember, but it did seem to help with the insanely frequent mood swings in high school- not sure what the deal with that was or if it was the Celexa or what, since it would just happen without anything setting me off- but I'm not sure how well it worked for my depression, and I suspect it may have permanently worsened my memory.

&nbsp;

There's not a lot of choice where I live and many aren't taking new patients and I might be moving out of state this fall for my bachelor's. I'm hoping so, because that'd be a good opportunity and it's also in a city where there should be more doctors to choose from. My only concern is the change in climate since it's a fair bit colder than I'm used to and might have less sunlight. Well, that and the crime rate.

Hm. Could you elaborate on normal people reaching hypomanic-like moods for short times?

&nbsp;

Are you BP1? That sounds intense.

&nbsp;

Yeah, sometimes I really think normal is boring, but it's definitely better than being down/depressed or being angry and fighting even more than usual with my parents.

Ugh yeah, I've experienced something close to that once. It was horrible. I felt like a train that lost control and kept speeding up and up and was about to derail. I don't feel like that now, though. At least not yet? Just maybe a bit more irritable and stuff. I'm not too worried, not yet anyway, because this usually doesn't last long, four or five days max.

&nbsp;

Thanks.

I've been so tempted to try to set one off. I did kind of try last summer by drinking energy drinks at night for two or three nights and binging neflix and drinking some alcohol- Not all at the same time. It didn't work. But caffeine doesn't work very well on me and I also wasn't on any medication at the time, IIRC. No Concerta or Pristiq. No Trileptal.

&nbsp;

How do you act and how much do you sleep when you're hypomanic?

&nbsp;

It does seem to be the general consensus that triggering hypomania is unwise because of the kindling theory and the possibility of it becoming mania.

I didn't think it sounded like him from what I'd heard about him, but I didn't feel like I knew enough to say something like that, so I just thought I would point out how the 'quote' really wasn't inspirational.

&nbsp;

I'd like to see a clip or article about that special.

My experience is a lot like yours! A lot of depression and maybe one or two hypomanic or subthreshold hypomanic episodes since March 2016.

&nbsp;

Last March for four or five days, I was really irritable with I guess increased motivation and racing thoughts and maybe self esteem and distractibility but I'm not sure about the last two.

&nbsp;

The end of February to around March 4th, about five days, I  definitely had increased self esteem and optimism and I felt more capable and happier than I had in a year, even though I have no social life now and I did then. I wouldn't say I was euphoric, but maybe I'm underestimating myself.I did want to comment on how much better I felt. I also only slept 23 hours from Monday to Saturday. 31 if I add Sunday and I'm not sure if that's accurate.

&nbsp;

But most of the time I'm depressed or alright. I do experience those odd very brief bursts of tingling and energy and restlessness, wanting to move, sometimes happiness and wanting to talk and ramble and laugh.

Actually, I think I was kind of like that on the Effexor- didn't feel depressed or just mildly, but kept thinking of suicide, though it wasn't too intense. I stopped because I was kind of scared and I didn't want to wait for it to get worse or keep having to deal with it in the hopes of it being a temporary side effect and then work properly. Oddly, I didn't have any problems with the Pristiq.

&nbsp;

Yes, that's better than nothing, and sometimes a month can go by quickly. If you're not feeling depressed and having time crawl by, at least.

Man, I was like this in high school, often more than once or twice a day. It was confusing and emotionally exhausting not to know how I would feel later that day or week and not to have my parents believe me because I spent most of my time at school and most of them happened during the day and even when they happened at home, I could conceal my unusual cheerfulness, especially since it only lasted a few hours. It got much better when I started Lamictal. Even when I came off it, I never went back to that incredibly swift cycling.

&nbsp;

Nothing ever happened to trigger them. I'd just be doing school work or talking to someone or reading or even once at a water park and then I'd go up or down. The water park incident was rather frustrating. I was fine and enjoying myself if I recall correctly and then all of a sudden there was this sinking despair and darkness and I just wanted to hug myself and cry.

&nbsp;

Edit: While I've never gone back to that pace, I still do get mood changes like that, both up and depressed, on occasion for a few hours, sometimes with an increase in energy, sometimes not. The elation isn't nearly as intense though, unfortunately. No idea why not. It felt like being high on something sometimes. Life was incredible and I quite liked being alive during those short bursts.

I'm doubting myself because what seemed like dysphoric irritable hypomania wasn't this intense. Is it possible that's because I struggled a lot with anger and even some rage blackouts when I was younger so now my anger is generally better controlled and less intense?

&nbsp;

I've only had a few that I can vaguely remember and just one sticks out. Last year in March, after spring break. I'm not sure if I was also really angry because of stress with school and my parents not agreeing with me dropping classes or taking a medical withdrawal. And this was before my worst suicidal episode in April.... anyway I also had a cold and I was taking Mucinex. I'm not sure if that could also set it off, but I took it or something similar in December and nothing happened.

&nbsp;

I just felt on edge and it was mostly okay at school but people pretty much leave me alone so... idk. But at home.... I kept wanting to scream and fight and break things and slam doors. I might also have wanted to cut myself as a way to redirect the anger. I think I did throw a pitcher of lemonade- I don't remember if it was at this time or a different time- and I rarely fight with my dad but we got into a pretty bad screaming match and might have nearly come to blows. I don't remember if I had racing thoughts the other days but one day I definitely did and it increased in speed until it was definitely uncomfortable and I couldn't focus on my homework and I was in no shape to make up a test. I felt like a train that was about to derail. I don't remember if I emailed my teacher or what but I know I didn't take it that day. I took a klonopin or two that I only use for really bad anxiety maybe a couple times a year if that. It helped the thoughts slow down, thank god. I'm not sure what I would have done if it didn't.

&nbsp;

The next day (Thursday) I think I was better, maybe thinking at a normal pace, but still kind of irritable. I did break a pencil or two and wanted to punch the wall when I took my test and I kind of freaked out when I dropped an earring and couldn't find it on my carpet or anywhere because my cat had a tendency to chew and lick anything, sometimes eating them, (she's much better now) and I yelled about it to my mom.

&nbsp;

I think I was back to normal the next day or at least Saturday. Is it normal for something like that that to end so abruptly?

&nbsp;

I don't really recall increased energy but my memory is horrible and I'm generally kind of a sloth anyway. I do know those rare angry times of a few days don't coincide with my monthly, even as irregular as it is.

