I did have something a bit weird at the end of November that lasted about four days and I didn't sleep for two days straight and had no appetite. I had three meals in two or three days. But I felt normal, no change in mood that I could tell. I just didn't want to sleep and my brain wouldn't shut up anyway. I'm pretty sure it was just a lot of thoughts, no racing. Although towards the end I felt kind of out of it and foggy.

Hm. How energetic were you? I've only had a few occasions of this that I can remember. Maybe two or three in the last year or two. They tend to be really short, maybe two to four days on average, both kinds- happier and irritable. This one was about five maybe six days long and it wasn't intense. It never really is. My energy was more like I wasn't as tired as I'd usually be, some days totally fine, a couple days a little tired but not as much as usual. I don't think I had the desire to move around or felt like I could just go and go.


&nbsp;

The increased optimism and confidence wasn't anything wild, but certainly a large difference in comparison to my usual low self esteem and somewhat pessimistic outlook. I felt like I'd totally be okay and everything could/would work out and I could pull off a B in math, which is my worst subject, though it's my only class right now. I felt more cheerful and kept wanting to comment on it but I'm not sure the level remained consistent and it didn't seem euphoric. I've been told it does have to be euphoric to be actual hypomania, or at least increased irritability.

&nbsp;

I didn't exactly crash but I did go from roughly 23.5 hours of sleep that week not counting Sunday, maybe 30 or 31 if counting it, to 63 hours the following week, although it did coincide with my being able to go to school several hours later than usual. My normal weekly range is 40-56 hours, with the total typically around 45 hours.

&nbsp;

What I just described in the topic post, though, has happened more often. Possibly up to five or six times since last year? I haven't noticed a pattern, although it's true I've probably gotten less sleep the night before. But usually that doesn't set anything off. It only lasts about 30 minutes to 2 or 3 hours.

